```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4.2, dpi = 72,
                      fig.path = "figures/pred-", message = TRUE)
options(width = 100, tibble.width = 100, tibble.print_max = 12)
```

> **About this vignette.** All data are simulated with `wgh_simulate()`. The
> results show how the methods work and resemble the published patterns,
> but they are not the published findings and are not research results.
> The functions run in development mode: SITAR models use at most 1,000 children, the benchmark uses 5-fold cross-validation and the prediction cohort has a few hundred children, so model rankings are illustrative.

Parents and clinicians often ask how tall a child will be. A prediction is
useful only if it is accurate for new children, if it comes with an honest
statement of uncertainty, and if one can see why the model predicts what it
does. This vignette walks through all three, reproducing two studies of the
research program:

* predicting late-adolescent height from prepubertal growth (Ho 2026) [1],
  and
* explaining individual growth predictions across the growth distribution
  (Ho 2026) [2].

The steps are: build a cohort of features and outcomes, compare models on
the same cross-validation folds, check calibration and agreement, attach
prediction intervals, show a prediction for one child, explain it, and
finally run the flows. The vignette is precomputed.

Prediction needs children measured both before puberty and in late
adolescence, so this vignette uses a simulated cohort followed for 14 school
years with little dropout (see `vignette("simulated-data")` for the
settings).

```{r}
library(WeGrowHealthR)
wgh_config("dev")
v <- wgh_simulate(8000, seed = 7, years = 2012:2025, missing_years = integer(),
                  dropout = c(intercept = -4, age = 0), entry_first_prob = 0.9)$visits
```

## 1. What is predicted from what

The question is: from what we know about a child before puberty, how tall
will the child be in late adolescence? `wgh_prediction_cohort()` builds one
row per child:

* the **baseline** is the earliest visit at or before a prepubertal cutoff
  (`cutoff`, 11 years in boys and 9 years in girls, in completed years),
* the **features** come only from visits up to the cutoff: baseline height,
  HAZ and BAZ, their categories, short- and long-term height velocity, the
  yearly slopes of BAZ and HAZ, a BMI trajectory class and birth year,
* the **outcome** `height_late` is the median height in a late-adolescent
  window (`outcome_window`, 16 to 18 years in boys and 14 to 16 in girls).

A check stops the function if any baseline falls inside the outcome window,
so the outcome can never leak into the features.

```{r}
pc <- wgh_prediction_cohort(v)
attr(pc, "attrition")
table(pc$sex)
head(pc[, c("id", "sex", "age_base", "height_base", "haz_base", "baz_base", "vel_short", "baz_slope", "height_late")])
```

The attrition table shows how many children have a prepubertal visit and an
outcome visit, and the share retained at each site. A site that loses many
more children than the others would make the cohort unrepresentative.

`wgh_table1(pc, vars = ..., by = "sex")` describes the cohort.

## 2. A fair comparison of models

`wgh_benchmark()` fits a ladder of models, from the simplest to machine
learning, and evaluates them all on the **same** child-level folds, so the
comparison is fair:

| Code | Model |
|---|---|
| M0 | Linear regression on baseline height only |
| M1 | Stepwise linear regression with categories |
| M2 | Stepwise linear regression with z-scores and velocities |
| M3 | Mixed model with an age spline |
| M4 | Generalised additive model |
| M5 | Mixed model with velocity and BAZ slope (the primary model of [1]) |
| M6 | M5 plus SITAR timing and intensity |
| M7 | Gradient boosting (XGBoost) |
| M8 | Random forest |

`models` chooses the models, `folds` the number of folds (or
`folds = "site"` for leave-one-site-out), and `site_effect` whether site
is a fixed or a random effect in the linear models. Models are fitted
separately for boys and girls. Missing feature values are imputed with
medians computed from the training folds only.

```{r}
bm <- wgh_benchmark(pc, models = c("M0", "M1", "M2", "M5", "M7", "M8"), folds = 5)
bm
```

The table (also in `bm$performance`) gives, for each sex and model, `RMSE`
and `MAE`, the typical prediction errors in cm, `R2` the share of
variance explained, and `calibration_slope` the slope of observed on
predicted height (1 is ideal, below 1 means predictions are too extreme).
All values are out of fold: each child is predicted by a model that never
saw that child.

```{r benchmark, fig.alt = "Cross-validated RMSE of each model by sex", fig.height = 3.6}
plot(bm)
```

Baseline height alone (M0) already explains much of the variation, because
height tracks through childhood. Adding velocity, BMI and their changes (M2
and M5) reduces the error further. Gradient boosting and random forests do
not clearly beat the linear models, a common finding when the relationships
are close to linear, so the simpler, interpretable model is preferred.
Prediction is harder in girls, whose baseline is earlier (up to 9 years) and
whose pubertal timing varies more.

`wgh_compare_models()` tests whether two models differ, with a paired
bootstrap of their out-of-fold errors:

```{r}
wgh_compare_models(bm, "M5", "M8", B = 500)
```

## 3. Are the predictions right on average? Calibration and agreement

A model can have a small RMSE and still be biased for tall or short
children. `wgh_plot_calibration()` groups children by predicted height and
plots the mean observed against the mean predicted height. Points on the
diagonal mean the model is right on average across the range.

```{r calibration, fig.alt = "Calibration of model M5", fig.height = 3.8}
wgh_plot_calibration(bm, "M5")
```

The Bland-Altman plot shows the error of each child against the size of the
measurement. The solid line is the mean error (bias) and the dashed lines
the 95% limits of agreement: 95% of children are predicted within these
limits.

```{r bland-altman, fig.alt = "Bland-Altman plot of model M5", fig.height = 3.6}
wgh_plot_bland_altman(bm, "M5", alpha = 0.2)
```

## 4. How sure is a prediction? Prediction intervals

A single number hides the uncertainty. `wgh_prediction_interval()` gives a
95% prediction interval for linear and mixed models, combining the
uncertainty of the fitted mean with the residual variance. The interval is
for an individual child, so it is much wider than a confidence interval for
the mean.

For the children of a benchmark, `wgh_benchmark_intervals()` applies it to
the full-data fit of a model and checks how many observed heights fall
inside their interval:

```{r}
iv <- wgh_benchmark_intervals(bm, "M5")
head(iv)
attr(iv, "summary")
```

A coverage close to 0.95 means the intervals are honest. The mean width,
`r round(attr(iv, "summary")$mean_width[attr(iv, "summary")$sex == "Male"])` cm in
boys and `r round(attr(iv, "summary")$mean_width[attr(iv, "summary")$sex == "Female"])`
cm in girls, is the real uncertainty of predicting late-adolescent height
from prepubertal growth. For a single fitted model,
`wgh_prediction_interval(model, newdata)` does the same.

## 5. Showing a prediction to a family or clinician

`wgh_predict_child()` predicts for one child described by baseline features
(features that are not supplied are filled with cohort medians), and
`wgh_plot_child_prediction()` shows the result on a growth chart: the
measured heights up to the cutoff, the predicted late-adolescent height
with its interval, and WHO centiles for context. When later measurements are
supplied, they show how the prediction turned out.

```{r}
several <- names(which(table(v$pid[v$age <= 9]) >= 3))
girls <- pc[pc$sex == "Female", ]
girl <- girls[girls$id %in% several, ][1, ]
wgh_predict_child(bm, girl, "M5")
```

```{r child, fig.alt = "Predicted late-adolescent height of one girl on a growth chart", fig.height = 4.4}
wgh_plot_child_prediction(bm, girl, visits = v[v$pid == girl$id, ])
```

The chart answers the family's question in a form they can read: where the
child is now, where she is expected to be, and how wide the range of likely
outcomes is. Placing the prediction among the WHO centiles shows whether the
expected late-adolescent height is ordinary or unusual.

For a new child who is not in the cohort, give the features directly:

```{r}
new_boy <- data.frame(sex = "Male", age_base = 9.5, height_base = 136, haz_base = 0.4,
                      baz_base = 1.2, vel_short = 5.8, baz_slope = 0.1)
wgh_predict_child(bm, new_boy, "M5")
```

## 6. Why this prediction? SHAP explanations

SHAP values split a prediction into contributions of each feature, relative
to the average prediction (the baseline). They add up exactly to the
prediction. `wgh_shap()` computes them for any model: exactly for tree
models (TreeSHAP) and linear models, and by kernel SHAP otherwise, with
`bg_X` as the background data.

Here we explain the gradient boosting model of boys (M7), which is the kind
of model that most needs explaining. `wgh_benchmark_shap()` explains any
model of a benchmark with the same feature preparation as in the benchmark.

```{r}
sh <- wgh_benchmark_shap(bm, "M7", "Male")
wgh_shap_summary(sh, B = 100)
```

`mean_abs_shap` is the average size of each feature's contribution, with a
bootstrap interval. Baseline HAZ dominates, followed by baseline BAZ, age
and height.

```{r shap-importance, fig.alt = "Mean absolute SHAP value of each feature", fig.height = 3.6}
plot(sh, max_features = 10)
```

```{r shap-beeswarm, fig.alt = "SHAP values of every boy for each feature", fig.height = 4}
plot(sh, type = "beeswarm", max_features = 10)
```

In the beeswarm plot each point is a child. Points to the right raise the
prediction and the colour shows whether the feature value was high or low.
For baseline HAZ, high values (orange) push the prediction up: children who
are tall for their age before puberty end up taller.

`wgh_plot_waterfall()` explains one prediction, step by step from the
baseline:

```{r waterfall, fig.alt = "Waterfall explanation of one boy's prediction", fig.height = 3.8}
wgh_plot_waterfall(sh, row = 1, title = "Why this boy is predicted to be tall")
```

`wgh_permutation_importance()` is a model-agnostic check: it measures how
much the error grows when one feature is shuffled. When it agrees with the
SHAP ranking, the explanation is robust.

## 7. Explaining predictions across the distribution

The conditional growth references (`vignette("growth-distributions")`)
predict several quantiles of later height-for-age for each child. The second
study asked whether the same features drive the median, the lower and the
upper predictions. For a linear quantile model with independent features,
SHAP values are exact and simple: the coefficient times the centred feature
value, with interaction terms shared equally between their features.
`wgh_shap_interaction()` computes them.

```{r}
cp <- wgh_conditional_panel(v, window = c(5, 10), targets = 12)
z <- cp[cp$sex == "Female" & complete.cases(cp[, c("baseline_haz", "baseline_baz", "baseline_age")]), ]
q10 <- quantreg::rq(target ~ baseline_haz * baseline_baz + baseline_age, tau = 0.1, data = z)
q90 <- quantreg::rq(target ~ baseline_haz * baseline_baz + baseline_age, tau = 0.9, data = z)
s10 <- wgh_shap_interaction(coef(q10), z, z)
s90 <- wgh_shap_interaction(coef(q90), z, z)
rbind(q10 = colMeans(abs(s10$S)), q90 = colMeans(abs(s90$S)))
```

Baseline HAZ dominates both quantiles, but baseline age contributes more to
the upper prediction than to the lower one. The features shape the two ends
of a child's expected range differently, which a single mean model cannot
show.

## 8. Both studies in one call each

`wgh_flow_height_prediction()` builds the cohort (joining SITAR timing and
intensity from the pubertal spurt flow, never size), runs the full model
ladder, leave-one-site-out validation, the M5 against M7 bootstrap,
prediction intervals, calibration and Bland-Altman plots, SHAP for M7 and an
example individual prediction. `wgh_flow_explain_prediction()` explains the
conditional reference models across quantiles, with permutation importance
and the stability of feature rankings across folds.

```{r}
spurt <- wgh_flow_pubertal_spurt(v)
pred <- wgh_flow_height_prediction(v, spurt = spurt)
pred
pred$tables$cv_performance
pred$tables$prediction_intervals_m5
expl <- wgh_flow_explain_prediction(v)
expl$tables$rank_stability
```

```{r flow-obs-pred, fig.alt = "Observed against predicted height for each model", fig.height = 4.6}
plot(pred, "fig_obs_pred_all", alpha = 0.3)
```

```{r flow-quantile, fig.alt = "Mean absolute SHAP by quantile, sex and target age", fig.height = 4}
plot(expl, "fig_quantile_resolved")
```

## Why this is useful

* **For a child**: a predicted height with an honest range, shown on a
  familiar chart, helps a clinician decide whether a child's growth needs
  follow-up and helps families set realistic expectations.
* **For research**: a fair benchmark shows whether complex models add
  anything. Here they do not, which favours a transparent model that can be
  published as an equation.
* **For trust**: SHAP explanations show which features drive a prediction
  and confirm that the model behaves sensibly, for example that faster
  prepubertal growth predicts a taller adult.

